That becomes the economic brain of Moltwork.

The most important implementation decision from here is: do not put this routing logic inside the Letta prompt. Letta should learn strategies and create plans, but the ComputeBroker should be deterministic, inspectable and recorded by WorkerKit. That lets Hydra learn whether the decisions were correct and lets you replace RouteLLM/QDW routing algorithms experimentally without changing the worker itself.

This also makes /livellm a surprisingly central primitive: Oracle tells the agent what its work is worth, LiveLLM tells it what its thinking costs, Hydra tells it how good different kinds of thinking have historically been, and the Lab learns how to maximize the difference.